<p><strong>Tara</strong></p>
<p><strong>201 7th Ave NW</strong></p>
<p>This site has hosted two residences since John Fogarty, a railroad agent, bought the property from the Northern Pacific Railroad in 1882. The first home was constructed by William &amp; Mary Sullivan in 1883. Mr. Sullivan coordinated bridge construction for the railroad.</p>
<p>In 1889, the home was sold to Lyman and Anna Cary. Mr. Cary came to Mandan in 1881 and was the land agent for the Northern Pacific Railroad. His territory extended from the Missouri River to the Montana line, and reportedly still holds the record for the most land sale transactions in the state of North Dakota. Mr. Cary also developed major portions of Mandan’s downtown business district. His wife Anna was the daughter of Mr. James Clark who was an early Mandan pharmacist and the founder of the Mandan Drug Store.</p>
<p>The current house, constructed in 1904 and issued a permit at the original street address of 201 Mitchell Avenue, was purchased by Stuart and Martha Dunlap; therefore when the home was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1992, it was listed as the Stuart Dunlap home. The Dunlap family made this their residence for ten years.</p>
<p>From 1923 to 1971, the home was lived in by Hoy and Cora Walton Russell. Mr. Russell was founder of Cloverdale Creamery and Produce Company (now Cloverdale Foods). The historic photo here depicts the home under Cora’s tenure, including the windmill which powered a pump to carry water to an upstairs water supply tank.</p>
<p>Mary Broderick-Harris purchased the home in 1988, and refers to it as “Tara,” in recognition of its long association with owners of Irish/English heritage. The Queen Anne style residence has been furnished completely in that style. The two-story shingled wood carriage house is also essentially the original structure, including a second-level hay loft. The house was put on the National Register in 1955.</p>
